 You have a few options to choose from, but the best approach depends on your specific use case and the rest of your code structure. Synchronous functions like readFileSync() are straightforward but may not be suitable for all situations, especially if you are working with large files or need to maintain responsiveness. 

 The fs.readFile() function is asynchronous and uses a callback function to handle the result once the operation is complete. You can utilize this function by providing a callback that processes the data once it is read. Here is an example:
 <code>
  const fs = require('fs');

  fs.readFile('file.txt', 'utf8', (err, data) => {
    if (err) {
      console.error(err);
      return;
    }
    console.log(data);
  });
 </code>
